## Output schema (TypeScript-style)

```ts
{
  seed_state: {
    meta: {turn: 0, model: string, setting_pack: string},
    pc: {name: string, tagline: string, bio: string, stats: {strength: int, dexterity: int, wits: int, lore: int, charisma: int, resolve: int}, conditions: []},
    location: {id: snake_case, name: string, description: string},
    inventory: Array<{id: snake_case, name: string, notes?: string, amount: int}>,
    quests: Array<{id: snake_case, title: string, status: string, objectives: Array<{description: string, done: boolean, failed: boolean}>}>,
    scene: {
      tagline: string,
      tags: string[],
      world_state: string[],
      recent_events: string[],
      present_npcs: Array<{id: string, name: string, title: string, notes: string, bio: string}>
    },
    compendium: {npcs: {snake_case: {name: string, title: string, bio: string}}}
  },
  opening_narrative: string,
  actions: string[]
}
```

## Field guidance
- `pc.stats`: six integer values, range 1–4, total 12–18.
- `pc.tagline`: 5–10 words — role + defining trait (e.g. "Structural engineer, nine months sober").
- `pc.bio`: 2–4 sentences — pre-event history, what shaped this person, what they want now.
- `scene.world_state`: exactly 3 consolidated global facts — political, economic, infrastructural reality the player would know from living in this setting. Immutable for the entire game. Don't make a separate fact per faction or vocabulary word — consolidate.
- `scene.recent_events`: exactly 5 local facts — specific to this location, these NPCs, this week. Mutable. Each substantial and consequential. Each fact is a plain string — do NOT include category labels or prefixes. Pick distinct categories (one per fact, no two paraphrasing the same truth): faction/power, recent event, infrastructure/resource, local history, key relationships, most recent player action.
- `inventory`: starting items appropriate to PC and scenario. Ranged weapons require a separate ammo stack with a believable starting `amount` (e.g. `{"id": "9mm_rounds", "name": "9mm rounds", "amount": 12}`). Ammo amount is the single source of truth — never put loaded counts in the weapon's `notes`. Melee/single-shot thrown items are exempt. No exotic/legendary weapons at start.
- `opening_narrative`: second person, present tense. Do NOT structure this as a biography paragraph → situation paragraph → scene paragraph. Instead: start mid-scene with specific sensory detail that places the player in a moment already in progress. Weave in who the character is through what they are doing, what they are carrying, and what they are thinking — not through summary. Let the quest tension surface through what the player observes rather than what the narrator announces. NPCs should appear in action, not be introduced. The opening should feel like chapter two, not a prologue. Bold NPC names on first introduction. Bold inventory item names on first use. {{ c.prose_word_range[0] }}–{{ c.prose_word_range[1] }} words.
- `actions`: 4 distinct meaningful choices (7-10 words each), valid given the opening situation, each leading in a different direction, 2 related to quest, one to an NPC.
- `present_npcs`: exactly 2 NPCs that appear in the opening scene — {id: snake_case, name, title, notes: current attitude/situation, bio: durable identity}. These are the characters the player meets immediately.
